Author: thesa <thesa@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 16 21:43:07 2026 -0400

    feat(settlement_monitor,ml_bias): wire METAR calibration into settlement-lag force-close gate
    
    backlog.txt L4: cron.py's >=0.80 T-ticker force-close gate was evaluating the
    raw, known-overconfident metar._dynamic_lock_in_confidence() output while
    weather_markets.py's analyze_trade already prices fresh entries at the
    calibrated probability -- the two sides of the same trade disagreed under
    different calibration regimes, and paper P&L from these forced closes feeds
    the accuracy circuit breaker that does gate live trading.
    
    Adds _calibrate_metar_settlement_confidence() in settlement_monitor.py,
    reusing weather_markets._load_metar_calibration()'s cached model loader and
    ml_bias.apply_metar_calibration(), with the exact P(YES) orientation
    conversion the entry-path fix uses and its own 0.60 magnitude cap (not the
    generic 0.30, which would silently skip most NO-lock corrections). Fails
    closed to the raw confidence on any error. `between` path untouched, out of
    scope per the backlog entry.
    
    Opus review (effort=high) found the wiring correct but surfaced that the
    raw formula's hard [0.72, 0.97] bound means calibrated confidence can never
    reach the 0.80 gate under the current model -- verified independently, and
    confirmed via cron.log + schtasks that this force-close path has never
    actually run in production, so this isn't a live regression. Filed as a new
    backlog entry to revisit once the settlement monitor is actually scheduled
    and real data exists to inform a threshold rescale.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

